WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
How can a record full of gaps still show that life changed?

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.540
A fossil scallop rests inside a Virginia riverbank.

3
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.280
Above it, a modern river crosses land that was once beneath a shallow sea.

4
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:20.933
A fossil is the preserved remains or traces of past life.

5
00:00:22.413 --> 00:00:25.153
Shells, bones, teeth, and footprints can become fossils.

6
00:00:26.613 --> 00:00:28.253
Most remains rot or are eaten.

7
00:00:29.513 --> 00:00:31.913
Hard parts and quick burial improve the chance.

8
00:00:33.153 --> 00:00:34.413
The chance stays small.

9
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:38.433
All fossils found so far make the fossil record.

10
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:42.833
Because fossilization is rare, that record has gaps.

11
00:00:44.093 --> 00:00:45.853
A gap means some details are missing.

12
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:49.553
It does not make the fossils beside it disappear.

13
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.053
The pattern can survive.

14
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:57.227
In undisturbed layers, read from older below to younger above.

15
00:00:58.507 --> 00:01:00.467
Some kinds appear only in older rock.

16
00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:03.427
Some continue in changed forms.

17
00:01:04.927 --> 00:01:09.247
Sea shells inside inland rock also record an environment that is gone.

18
00:01:10.947 --> 00:01:12.287
Together, changes build history.

19
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.950
Now remove one band of rock between two fossil layers.

20
00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:19.970
The lower layer holds one form.

21
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.110
The upper layer holds a changed form.

22
00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.130
Does the missing band erase the pattern we can observe?

23
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:29.690
Think before you decide.

24
00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:40.143
No. We can still compare the fossils on both sides.

25
00:01:41.383 --> 00:01:44.163
The gap limits what we know between them, so keep it gray.

26
00:01:45.843 --> 00:01:50.463
Evidence stays honest when the picture shows both observations and uncertainty.

27
00:01:52.103 --> 00:01:53.103
Limits stay visible.

28
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:57.653
Different groups also appear at different places on a time strip.

29
00:01:59.193 --> 00:02:04.113
Fish, four-legged animals, mammal relatives, and bird-like dinosaurs have well-known

30
00:02:04.113 --> 00:02:09.353
early examples. They belong on separate branches, not one ladder.

31
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:12.433
They are examples, not ancestors.

32
00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:18.313
Fossils record existence, extinction, change, and past environments.

33
00:02:19.713 --> 00:02:22.653
Each observation supports some claims and not others.

34
00:02:23.973 --> 00:02:25.813
A shell can place life in an old sea.

35
00:02:27.053 --> 00:02:29.073
It cannot name every branch by itself.

36
00:02:30.513 --> 00:02:31.773
Evidence needs clear scope.

37
00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:38.483
In science, a theory is a well-tested explanation backed by many lines of evidence.

38
00:02:40.183 --> 00:02:43.583
Evolution explains why populations change and share ancestors.

39
00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:48.123
It also makes predictions that new evidence can test.

40
00:02:49.243 --> 00:02:50.483
That makes it testable.

41
00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:53.527
Return to the Virginia scallop.

42
00:02:54.687 --> 00:02:57.607
Its layer records a living thing and an ancient sea.

43
00:02:58.907 --> 00:03:04.007
Its resemblance to living scallops invites another question: which organisms share

44
00:03:04.007 --> 00:03:08.327
branches? Bones and sequences can test that next.

45
00:03:09.447 --> 00:03:10.667
The question continues.

46
00:03:11.817 --> 00:03:15.377
The record is incomplete, so every claim keeps its limits.

47
00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:19.077
Still, observed fossils remain evidence.

48
00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:24.557
Across many layers, they show that life and environments changed through time.
